
{"id":"494880e2-ecf5-491f-8ac1-20d8ad7208b3","object":"chat.completion","created":1784961498,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that returns {\"removed_node_ids\": [...], \"retained_node_ids\": [...]}. The case has nodes with properties: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also required_symbols.\n\nThe task: grammar_guided_node_reduction: reduce a parse tree (nodes form a tree via parent references) while preserving an interestingness oracle (represented by required_symbols: the tree must contain all required symbols after removal). The mechanism is similar to Perses syntax-guided reduction: try to delete removable nodes (with certain constraints) and also possibly replace? But the description mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". However, the output only has removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids, so we are only removing nodes, not replacing. Replacement might be handled by removing a node and its subtree? But then we need to retain the children? The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove a node if it has retained children? That suggests we may remove a node and all its descendants (since children are removed if parent removed). But then \"retained children\" meaning children that are not being removed? Actually, the rule: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we consider removing a node (i.e., deleting it and its entire subtree) and check if the remaining tree still has all required symbols. If yes, we can remove it. But we need to ensure we process deeper nodes first (depth descending). Also use node ID as tie break at equal depth (maybe lexicographic?).\n\nThe output: removed_node_ids ordered by actual removal order? The description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be in order of removal (i.e., the order in which we decide to remove nodes). retained_node_ids should be sorted (presumably ascending lexicographic). \n\nWe need to build a tree from nodes. Each node has parent (string or null for root). Build adjacency: child nodes. Also we need to know which nodes are removable. Required symbols: list of symbol strings that must be present in the tree after removal. The root is always present and is not removable.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Build a dict node_by_id.\n2. Build children list for each parent.\n3. Determine initial set of nodes that are retained (all nodes). But we will iteratively remove removable nodes.\n\nWe need to process nodes in order: deeper depth first, then shallower. However, after removing a node, its descendants are removed as well. So we need to process in a way that we consider removal of a node only if its descendants have already been considered? But the description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" This suggests we iterate over nodes sorted by depth descending, and for each node, if it is removable and not already removed, we try to remove it. If removal is feasible (i.e., after removing it and its subtree, all required symbols are still present in the remaining tree), we remove it (mark as removed) and also remove its entire subtree (if not already removed). This is like a greedy reduction: top-down? Actually deeper first: we start from the deepest nodes, so when we try to remove a deep node, its descendants are already considered? But if we remove a node, its descendants are removed as well, so we should process from deepest to shallowest, so when we consider a shallower node, its descendants may have already been removed, leaving it as a leaf. That matches typical Perses: try to delete nodes bottom-up.\n\nBut the rule \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" means if a node still has children that are retained (i.e., not removed), we cannot remove it. So we must process such that we only remove nodes after all their children have been either removed or decided to stay? Actually if we process bottom-up, when we consider a node, its children have already been processed (considered for removal). If some children were kept (not removed), then the node has retained children, so it cannot be removed. If all children were removed, then it becomes leaf and can be considered for removal. This matches.\n\nAlso \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So each time we consider removing a node, we check if the set of symbols in the remaining tree (excluding the subtree of this node) still contains all required symbols.\n\nWe also need to handle the root: never removed, and removable=false anyway. Also nodes with removable=false cannot be removed. So we ignore them.\n\nTie-breaking: at same depth, use node ID as stable tie break. So sort nodes by depth descending, then by node ID (string) ascending, perhaps. But the order might affect which node gets removed first if multiple are possible? The algorithm must be deterministic. The order of iteration determines removal order. So we sort nodes by (-depth, node_id).\n\nWe need to maintain a set of removed node IDs. Initially empty. For each node in sorted order (deepest first), if node is removable and not removed, we simulate removal: we need to check if all required symbols are still present in the tree after removing this node and its subtree. But note that some descendants may already be removed, so the subtree that would be removed is only the node itself and its currently retained descendants. However, if we remove the node, we must also remove any retained descendants because they are in its subtree. So we need to collect all nodes in the subtree that are not already removed. We can compute a set of nodes to be removed if we remove this node: the node plus all its retained descendants (those not already removed). Then we compute the set of symbols that would remain: all symbols from nodes that are not removed and not in this set. Check if that set includes all required_symbols. If yes, then we remove this node and all its retained descendants (add them to removed set). Record the node ID in removal order.\n\nBut careful: If we remove a node, its children are also removed. But those children may have already been considered for removal earlier (since deeper). So when we get to a shallower node, its descendants may be already removed, so the subtree is empty. So we check condition.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Parse case['nodes'] into list of dicts.\n- Build dict id->node.\n- Build dict parent->list of children ids.\n- Create set of all node ids.\n- Create set of required_symbols.\n- Determine depth from parent? The nodes have depth field. Good.\n- Sort nodes by (-depth, id). But also we need to know which are removable.\n- removed = set()\n- removal_order = []\n- For each node in sorted list:\n  - if node['id'] in removed: continue\n  - if not node['removable']: continue\n  - # compute subtree to remove (descendants that are not already removed)\n  - subtree_ids = get_subtree(node['id']) but only including ids not yet removed.\n  - # compute remaining symbols: from all nodes except those in removed union subtree_ids\n  - check if all required_symbols present.\n  - if yes:\n      # perform removal\n      removal_order.append(node['id'])\n      removed.update(subtree_ids) # includes node itself\n- After loop, retained = all_ids - removed\n- Sort retained lexicographically.\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removal_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained}\n\nNeed function get_subtree: recursively collect all descendants that are not already removed. Could do DFS from node_id, skip nodes that are in removed set. But note that if a child is already removed, we don't include it. So we traverse only retained children.\n\nWe need to build children adjacency beforehand.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Use a set `removed` as a global variable.\n- get_subtree function: given node_id, collect node_id and for each child (from children dict) if child not in removed, recurse. But careful: if we are removing the node, we are adding all descendants to removed, but some may have been considered earlier. That's fine.\n\nEdge cases: The root is not removable, so not considered. But required_symbols includes 'root'? In examples, yes. Root symbol is 'root'.\n\nMake sure to handle empty nodes list? But always at least root.\n\nAlso note: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So after removal, the remaining nodes must include all required symbols. In the condition, we check if the set of symbols from non-removed nodes (excluding subtree) includes all required.\n\nAlso, we need to compute symbols from non-removed nodes. We can precompute a map of node_id->symbol. Then during check, we can iterate over all nodes that are not removed and not in subtree, collect symbols. But that's O(N) per removal, could be O(N^2). Since N small (fixtures have 7 nodes), okay.\n\nAlternatively, maintain a count of required symbols present. When we remove a node, we need to ensure that the required symbols are still present in the rest. But since we also remove entire subtree, we need to check if any of the required symbols are only present in that subtree. So we can check: for each required_symbol, is there a node outside the removed set and outside the subtree that has that symbol? If for any required_symbol, no such node, then removal invalid. This can be done by iterating over required_symbols and checking.\n\nBetter: compute set of symbols in subtree: symbols_subtree = {node['symbol'] for node in subtree_ids if id in node dict}. Then compute set of symbols in entire tree (excluding removed already). Then check if (symbols_remaining - symbols_subtree) still contains all required. But careful: symbols_remaining already excludes removed. So if a required symbol only appears in the subtree (and nowhere else), then removal invalid.\n\nSo we can compute:\n\nsymbols_remaining = set of symbols from nodes not in removed.\nsymbols_subtree = set of symbols from nodes in subtree_ids (including node itself).\nif all(req_sym in symbols_remaining - symbols_subtree for req_sym in required_symbols): condition passes.\n\nBut note: if a required symbol appears both inside subtree and outside, removal is fine because symbol still present outside.\n\nThus algorithm.\n\nWe also need to handle the case where a node is removable but its parent may have been removed already. In that case, the node is already in removed set, so we skip.\n\nOne more point: The order of removal: we record node IDs as we remove them. Since we process deepest first, the removal order will be bottom-up. The description says \"actual removal order\" which should be the order we decide to remove them.\n\nNow, test with the given fixture cases. Let's simulate mentally for first case (b-000). Nodes:\nroot (depth0, removable false) symbol root\nn1 (depth1, removable false) symbol s1\nn2 (depth1, removable true) symbol s2\nn3 (depth1, removable false) symbol s0\nn4 (depth2, n2 child, removable true, symbol s1)\nn5 (depth2, n3 child, removable false, symbol s2)\nn6 (depth2, n1 child, removable true, symbol s0)\nrequired_symbols: root, s1\n\nSorted by depth desc, tie ID: depth2: n4, n5, n6; depth1: n1, n2, n3; depth0 root (skip). Also n5 is not removable, skip.\n\nProcess n4 (removable true, not removed). Subtree: n4 itself (no children? n4 has no children? Based on nodes, n4 is leaf? Yes, no node has parent n4, so leaf). Symbols_subtree = {s1}. Symbols_remaining: all nodes symbols: root, s1 (n1), s2 (n2), s0 (n3), s1 (n4), s2 (n5), s0 (n6) -> set {root, s1, s2, s0}. After removing n4, symbols_remaining - {s1} = {root, s2, s0}. required={root, s1}. s1 is not present -> invalid. So cannot remove n4.\n\nProcess n5: not removable, skip.\n\nProcess n6 (removable true). n6 leaf, symbol s0. Symbols_subtree={s0}. Removing it leaves {root,s1,s2,s1(?), actually n1 has s1, n2 s2, n3 s0, n4 s1, n5 s2}. But since s0 from n6 removed, still s0 present in n3 (s0). So symbols_remaining after removing n6: {root,s1,s2,s0} (n3 gives s0). required includes s1 and root, both present. So removal valid. Remove n6. removal_order = ['n6']; removed = {n6}.\n\nNow process depth1 nodes: n1 (not removable), n2 (removable true, not removed), n3 (not removable).\n\nProcess n2. Subtree: n2 and its descendants that are not removed. n2's children: n4 (not removed). So subtree_ids = {n2, n4}. Symbols_subtree: n2:s2, n4:s1 -> {s2,s1}. Symbols_remaining (from all not removed): root( root), n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n4(s1), n5(s2) -> {root,s1,s2,s0}. After removal: {root,s1,s0} (from n1,s1; n3,s0; n5,s2? n5 is not child of n2, so it remains, but n5 symbol s2 is removed from subtree? n5 is not in subtree because parent n3, not n2. So n5 remains, symbol s2. Wait, we need to compute symbols_remaining after removing subtree_ids: nodes not removed and not in subtree: root, n1, n3, n5. Their symbols: root, s1, s0, s2. So still have s1 and root and s2. But required only root and s1 - both present. So removal seems valid? But wait, required includes s1, and we have n1 with s1. So yes, we can remove n2 and n4. However, note that n4 had symbol s1, but n1 also has s1, so s1 still present. So we should remove n2 and n4. But is that allowed? \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" n2 has child n4, but n4 is being removed as part of subtree, so no retained children after removal. So valid. So removal of n2 is possible. Then removal_order becomes ['n6', 'n2']; removed = {n6, n2, n4}. Then process n3 (not removable) skip. End.\n\nRetained nodes: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted: ['n1', 'n3', 'n5', 'root']? But root is 'root', lexicographically 'n1' < 'n3' < 'n5' < 'root'? Actually string compare: 'n1' < 'n3' < 'n5' < 'root' because 'r' > 'n'. So retained_node_ids: ['n1','n3','n5','root'].\n\nRemoved: ['n2','n4','n6']? Wait removal order: first n6, then n2. So removed_node_ids: ['n6','n2'] but also n4 is removed automatically because n2 subtree includes n4. But should we list n4 in removed_node_ids? The schema says removed_node_ids is an array of strings. It should contain all node IDs that were removed. The \"actual removal order\" might be the order in which we explicitly removed nodes (i.e., the decision points). However, n4 is removed as consequence of removing n2. Should it be listed? The description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" It says \"the actual removal order\" for removed_node_ids. Typically, in Perses, when you delete a node, you delete the entire subtree. The removed_node_ids should list all nodes that are removed, in the order they are removed? Or only the \"heads\"? The problem statement: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" and \"removed_node_ids\" likely includes all nodes that were removed, not just the ones we explicitly decided. But the output from the schema expects an array of strings. The examples? We don't have expected outputs. We need to infer.\n\nLook at the mechanism: \"Tentatively remove a node only if ...\". So the decision is to remove a node. The removal of its descendants is implicit. So \"removed_node_ids\" might just include the nodes that were the heads of the removed subtrees (the nodes we decided to remove). But the phrase \"actual removal order\" could mean the order in which we apply the head removals. However, the schema says items type string: it doesn't specify if they are the heads or all. If we include all descendant nodes, we would need to maintain order of when they are effectively removed, which is when the head is removed. But for descendants that were previously considered and not removed, they get removed later when head is removed. That could be ambiguous.\n\nLet's examine the wording: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The \"actual removal order\" likely means the order in which we remove nodes (the decision points). The retained IDs are sorted. The removed_node_ids should be the IDs of nodes that are removed (all of them), but ordered by when they are removed. However, if we include all descendants, we need to decide order. For example, if we remove n2, we remove n2 and n4. The order: n2 is removed first? Then n4? But n4 is part of the same operation. The typical output might list only the head node (n2) as removed, and n4 is implicitly gone and appears in retained? No, it's not retained. The retained list includes only nodes that remain. So removed_node_ids should include n4 as well. But then we need to include it in removal order. One could list all nodes in the subtree in topological order (parent before child). But the spec does not specify. Let's read: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" and \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" It seems the removal order is the order in which nodes are tentatively removed. That is, we only record the head nodes. The descendant nodes are consumed and are not individually removed; they are just omitted from retained. But they are also \"removed\". However, if we list only heads, then the removed set may not include all nodes that are not retained. But the schema says removed_node_ids should contain all nodes that are removed. To avoid ambiguity, I think it's safer to include all nodes that are ultimately removed (i.e., all node IDs not in retained). The order could be the order in which they are removed: first all nodes that are part of the first head's subtree (in DFS order), then the next head's subtree, etc. But the description doesn't specify ordering within a subtree. However, we can derive from process: when we remove a head node, we remove its entire subtree (which may already have some nodes removed from earlier heads? Actually earlier heads are deeper, so they may have already removed some descendants. So for a given head, we add to removed set the node and any retained descendants. The order of adding to removal_order could be: add the head node first, then add its descendants (in depth-first order). But the spec says \"the actual removal order\" which might simply be the order we decide to remove the head nodes. I think the intended output is that removed_node_ids contains exactly the node IDs of the nodes that we decided to remove (the heads), not all descendants. Because the phrase \"actual removal order\" suggests a sequence of decisions, not all individual nodes. Moreover, in many reduction algorithms, you report which nodes were deleted (the ones you chose). The retained list includes all that are left. The removed are the ones you selected for deletion. The children are removed as side effect, but they are not separate decisions. The fact that retained list is sorted and removed list is in removal order supports that removed are the decision nodes. Because if we included all descendants, the removal order would be a long list mixing sub-structures.\n\nLet's check the output schema: it doesn't specify. But the problem statement: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" with \"registered_candidate_order\" and \"stable_ties\". The \"candidate_order\" likely refers to the order in which we consider nodes for removal. The output includes \"removed_node_ids\" which are the nodes actually removed (the candidates that succeeded). I think it's the head nodes.\n\nTo be safe, we can implement to output only the head nodes in removal order (the ones we tentatively remove). That matches typical Perses reporting. Also, the description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained are sorted, removed are in order. That indicates removed are a subset of the node IDs, not all non-retained. Because if retained are all remaining nodes, then the union of retained and removed would be all nodes. So removed must be the complement of retained. If we only output heads, then the complement of retained is not exactly heads; it includes descendants. So that would violate that union equals all node IDs. Therefore, removed_node_ids must include all nodes that are not retained. Because the output is supposed to be a complete partition: the tree after reduction consists exactly of the retained nodes, and the removed nodes are all others. So if we only list heads, the retained set would be wrong (they are not all nodes). So we must output all removed node IDs. So we need to list all node IDs that are not in retained. The order? \"actual removal order\" might mean the order in which nodes were removed (including descendants when their parent is removed). That would be more complex. But maybe we can output the removed IDs in any order? The schema just requires an array; it doesn't specify order. But the description says \"actual removal order\". So we need to provide an ordering that reflects the process. I'll assume we need to order them in the sequence of removal: first, when we remove a head node, we remove that node and then its descendants (in some consistent order). Then next head, etc. Since we process from deepest to shallowest, and when we remove a head, its subtree may contain nodes that were previously considered and not removed (because they were not heads). Those descendants will now be removed as part of the head's removal. They should appear in the removal order after the head? Or before? The process: we remove the head (add to removal_order) and then we remove all its retained descendants. But we could add them in some order (e.g., depth-first). However, the \"actual removal order\" might be the order in which we mark them as removed (i.e., when we decide to remove the head, all its subtree are removed simultaneously). In that case, the head could be considered the first among the subtree. I think a reasonable approach: for each head removal, append the head node ID, then recursively append its descendants' IDs in depth-first order (child before grandchild, but since deeper nodes are processed first in the algorithm, they might already be removed? Actually when we process deeper nodes first, we might have already considered some descendants and not removed them (because they didn't meet criteria). Then later we remove their parent, which forces their removal. So they were not removed earlier because the condition wasn't met (maybe because they were needed for required symbols, but after removing the parent, the required symbols are still present elsewhere). So they become removed now. So we need to include them. So we can collect all nodes in the subtree that are not already removed, and append them in some order (e.g., DFS order from the head). Then we mark them removed.\n\nAlternatively, we can just compute the set of all nodes that are removed after the algorithm, sort them by something? But the description says \"actual removal order\", which suggests we need to know the sequence. I'll implement a removal order that respects: for each head we decide to remove, we add that head ID, then add IDs of its retained descendants in depth-first order (pre-order: node before its children). That seems natural.\n\nGiven the small sizes, this should be fine.\n\nNow, let's test the first case with this assumption. Head removals: n6, then n2. For n6: head and no descendants -> removal_order add 'n6'. For n2: head n2, then descendants: n4 (leaf) -> add 'n4'. So removal_order: ['n6','n2','n4']. Retained: root, n1, n3, n5 -> sorted: ['n1','n3','n5','root'].\n\nNow check if required symbols after removal: root and s1 from n1 remain, yes.\n\nNow we need to ensure that when we process n2, we compute its subtree: n2 and n4. Both not removed yet. So after removal, we add 'n2' then 'n4'.\n\nThat seems plausible.\n\nNow, implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nWe'll need to handle multiple test cases. The function will be called with the payload object.\n\nImplementation steps in solve(case):\n- nodes = case['nodes']\n- required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n- build id_to_node, children dict.\n- all_ids = set(id_to_node.keys())\n- removed = set()\n- removal_order = []\n\n- def collect_subtree(node_id):\n    # returns list of ids in subtree that are not already removed, in DFS order (node first)\n    if node_id in removed:\n        return []\n    result = [node_id]\n    for child_id in children.get(node_id, []):\n        result.extend(collect_subtree(child_id))\n    return result\n\n- candidate_nodes = sorted([node for node in nodes if node['removable'] and node['id'] not in removed], key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n- For each node in candidate_nodes:\n    if node['id'] in removed: continue\n    subtree_list = collect_subtree(node['id'])\n    if not subtree_list: continue (should not happen)\n    # compute symbols in subtree\n    symbols_subtree = set(id_to_node[sub_id]['symbol'] for sub_id in subtree_list)\n    # compute symbols in remaining tree (all nodes not removed)\n    remaining_symbols = set(id_to_node[nid]['symbol'] for nid in all_ids if nid not in removed)\n    # check if after removal, all required symbols remain\n    remaining_after = remaining_symbols - symbols_subtree\n    if all(req in remaining_after for req in required_symbols):\n        # removal valid\n        removal_order.extend(subtree_list)  # head first, then children\n        removed.update(subtree_list)\n\n- retained_ids = sorted(all_ids - removed)\n- return {\"removed_node_ids\": removal_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nPotential issue: When we process n2, we had previously removed n6, which changed the tree. The collect_subtree function should only include nodes not in removed. So for n2, it includes n2 and n4 (n4 not removed). Good.\n\nBut note: When we process n4 first, it was not removed. Later, n2 removal includes n4. But n4 was already considered and not removed because condition failed. That is correct.\n\nNow, we need to ensure we handle the root and non-removable nodes correctly.\n\nNow, test with the second case (b-001). Similar structure, just different utilities. Required symbols same. Let's see if removal decisions differ. But we don't have expected output, so we just need to ensure algorithm is consistent.\n\nEdge cases: If a node has a required symbol that is only present in its subtree, removal invalid. If multiple nodes have same symbol, removal might be possible.\n\nAlso, need to consider that after removal of a parent, some grandchildren symbols may become unavailable, but condition checks all required symbols present elsewhere.\n\nNow, about \"never remove a node with retained children\". Our algorithm implicitly handles that because if a node has retained children, then those children were not removed earlier (likely because they were non-removable or removal failed). In collect_subtree, we will include those children. When we evaluate condition, we are removing them as well. So that violates \"never remove a node with retained children\". The rule says we cannot remove a node if it has retained children. So we must enforce that before considering removal. The condition should be: the node must have no children that are retained (i.e., not removed and not being removed in this operation). But if we are removing the entire subtree, then no children remain, so it's okay. The rule \"never remove a node with retained children\" means we cannot remove a node if it still has children that are not being removed. In our process, we only remove the node when we are removing all its descendants as well. So it's fine. However, if a child is not removable and we try to remove the parent, then that child would be retained? Actually if the child is not removable, we cannot remove it. So removing the parent would leave that child with no parent? But the c